<ArticleLayout title="A Semantic Search API with FastAPI, Sentence-BERT and pgvector" description="Architecture and production checks for a semantic search API using FastAPI, Sentence-BERT embeddings and PostgreSQL pgvector." eyebrow="pgvector architecture" published="2025-10-21" updated="2026-10-13" legacy ctaTitle="Is vector search slowing a production workload?" ctaDescription="Review recall, filters, latency and PostgreSQL resource use together before choosing an index or a larger database instance.">
  <h2>The simple architecture</h2>
  <p>A FastAPI endpoint receives text, Sentence-BERT produces an embedding, and PostgreSQL stores the vector alongside document metadata. A query embeds the user's text with the same model, orders candidates by the chosen distance operator and returns the nearest documents. This is a useful proof of concept because the vector data stays close to relational filters and application records.</p>
  <p>The <a href="https://kiransabne.dev/create-a-semantic-search-api-with-fastapi-sentence-bert-and-postgresql-pgvector">original implementation walkthrough</a> covers the API build. This edition focuses on the checks needed when the demo becomes a service.</p>

  <h2>Production decisions beyond the API code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Embedding consistency:</strong> store the model name and version used for each vector. A model change may require re-embedding and index rebuilding; vectors from different models are not automatically comparable.</li>
    <li><strong>Distance and index:</strong> choose a distance metric that matches the model and benchmark exact search before adding approximate nearest-neighbor indexing. Compare HNSW and IVFFlat at a fixed recall target, not just peak query speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Filters:</strong> test the real tenant and metadata predicates. Approximate index scans may return too few results after filtering unless scan settings or index design are adjusted.</li>
    <li><strong>End-to-end latency:</strong> measure embedding generation, network time, SQL execution and result hydration separately. Optimizing only the database query can miss the largest cost.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For each important query cohort, maintain an exact-search result set as a recall baseline. Track latency, recall, returned-result count, memory and write/index-maintenance cost as data grows. The <a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector#readme">pgvector documentation</a> describes the index types and filtering behavior; our <a href="/insights/pgvector-hnsw-vs-ivfflat">HNSW versus IVFFlat guide</a> turns those details into a benchmark plan.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
